# Auth: accounts, password sign-in, and sessions

Generated by `skies g auth` with the Account module. The cases in `e2e/` drive the booted app over HTTP (an
isolated in-memory store per case) and were green when the blueprint was generated; keep them green as you change
the module, and add a failure mode here before changing behavior.

## Behavior

- `POST /account/register` creates an account for a globally unique, normalized email and an argon2id password of
  8 to 128 characters, and answers a taken email exactly as it answers a new one (nothing is created; the owner is
  told through `IAccountNotices`).
//<lz:tenancy>
- Every registration opens an org of its own; the account is its first member.
//</lz:tenancy>
- `POST /account/login` exchanges email + password for a short-lived access token (JWT) and a refresh token.
- `POST /account/refresh` rotates the refresh token; each login starts a family, and rotation stays in it.
- `POST /account/logout` revokes the family of the presented refresh token.
- `GET /account/me` returns the caller's own profile.
- `GET /account/sessions`, `POST /account/sessions/revoke`, and `POST /account/sessions/revoke-others` list and
  revoke the caller's live session families.
//<lz:tenancy>
- Users are tenant-scoped rows, but identity is global: email uniqueness and sign-in cross the org filter. A
  signed-in request acts in its token's org; an anonymous one resolves no org at all.
- A request acting in an org writes only that org's rows: inserting into another org, or updating or deleting
  another org's row, is refused before the save reaches the database.
//</lz:tenancy>
//<lz:cookies>
- A web client (`X-Client: web`) gets the refresh token in an httpOnly cookie instead of the body.
//</lz:cookies>
- Register, login, refresh, and logout are throttled per client address and endpoint (`CredentialRateLimit`).
- The app-admin policy (writes to app-wide data) needs the `Admin` role, which registration never grants.
- Outside Development the app refuses to start unless `Jwt:Secret` is configured with at least 32 bytes that are
  not the development key.

Before `skies proof run` or `record`, review each mode against the AVP catalog and add criteria or a reviewed
exemption. Generated code cannot make that applicability decision on behalf of the consuming app.

## Failure modes

- FM-[register] Registration does not produce a usable account: the email is stored un-normalized or the password does not verify
- FM-[invalid-email] A malformed email is accepted at registration
- FM-[duplicate] A taken email registers a second account, damages the first one, or answers differently from a new registration
- FM-[password-length] A password outside 8 to 128 characters is accepted at registration, or an over-long one is hashed at login
- FM-[missing-fields] A request that leaves a field out of its body fails as a server error instead of a client error
- FM-[register-signed-in] A registration sent with a valid access token fails as a server error or opens an account in the caller's name
//<lz:tenancy>
- FM-[register-org] A new account does not get an org of its own: two registrations share an org, or an account has none
//</lz:tenancy>
- FM-[login] Valid credentials do not yield a token pair whose access token reads the caller's own profile
- FM-[wrong-password] A wrong password signs in or leaks a token
- FM-[enumeration] Login reveals whether an email is registered: an unknown email and a wrong password answer differently
- FM-[login-throttle] One client can try passwords without limit: login is not throttled after the permitted attempts
//<lz:tenancy>
- FM-[global-login] A user cannot sign in when the request resolves to an org other than their own
//</lz:tenancy>
- FM-[profile-anonymous] The profile endpoint answers without an access token
- FM-[self-admin] A self-registered account satisfies the app-admin policy, and so may change app-wide data
- FM-[refresh-rotate] Refresh does not rotate: it returns the same refresh token or no usable access token
- FM-[refresh-replay] Replaying a rotated refresh token is accepted, or leaves the rest of its family alive
- FM-[refresh-unknown] An unknown refresh token is exchanged for tokens
- FM-[refresh-max-age] A session family older than the absolute maximum age still refreshes
- FM-[logout] After logout the session's refresh token still refreshes
- FM-[logout-unknown] Logout with an unknown token fails, revealing which tokens are valid
- FM-[sessions-list] The session list shows expired or rotated slots, misses a live session, or does not flag the current one
- FM-[revoke-session] Revoking one of my sessions leaves it alive or ends my other sessions
- FM-[revoke-foreign] A user can revoke another user's session, or learn that it exists
- FM-[revoke-others] Signing out everywhere else leaves other sessions alive, ends the current one, or touches another user's sessions
//<lz:tenancy>
- FM-[tenant-isolation] A read scoped to one org sees another org's rows
- FM-[anonymous-tenant] An anonymous request resolves to an org (a hidden default) and reads its rows
- FM-[tenant-writes] A request acting in one org inserts a row into another org, or updates or deletes another org's row
//</lz:tenancy>
//<lz:cookies>
- FM-[cookie-login] A web login returns the refresh token in the body, or without an httpOnly cookie
- FM-[cookie-refresh] A web refresh ignores the refresh cookie or does not reissue it
//</lz:cookies>

- FM-[production-config] The development database and providers can start outside Development
- FM-[jwt-secret] The app starts outside Development with a missing, short, or public development signing secret

## Out of scope

- Password strength beyond the length bounds, and lockout per account (the throttle is per client address).
- The throttle's window rolling over (the cases run well inside one window).
- Concurrent refreshes of one token racing on a relational database (the in-memory store cannot raise the
  concurrency conflict `auth.session_retry` reports).
